{"name":"Agave Frond","hex":"#5a6e6a","fetched":"2026-10-20","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"agave","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant in the large, variable genus Agave of succulent plants, commonly armed with formidable prickles, flowering at maturity after several years, and generally dying thereafter; large species, such as the maguey or century plant, (Agave americana), produce gigantic inflorescences. Several are of economic importance as sources of fibre such as sisal, and alcoholic beverages such as tequila."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/agave"},{"term":"frond","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The leaf of a fern, especially a compound leaf.","Any fern-like leaf or other object resembling a fern leaf."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frond"}],"entries":[{"title":"Agave","blocks":[{"text":"Agave is a genus of monocots native to the arid regions of the Americas. The genus is primarily known for its succulent and xerophytic species that typically form large rosettes of strong, fleshy leaves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Agave_americana_R01.jpg/330px-Agave_americana_R01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agave","wikidata":"Q155874"},{"title":"Frond","blocks":[{"text":"A frond is a large, divided leaf. In both common usage and botanical nomenclature, the leaves of ferns are referred to as fronds and some botanists restrict the term to this group. Other botanists allow the term frond to also apply to the large leaves of cycads, as well as palms (Arecaceae) and various other flowering plants, such as mimosa or sumac. \"Frond\" is commonly used to identify a large, compound leaf, but if the term is used botanically to refer to the leaves of ferns and algae it may be applied to smaller and undivided leaves. Whatever the term frond can also refer to structures or organisms of ediacaran lifeforms called Frondomorphs, which may be related to primitive animals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Davalia_frond_parts.png/330px-Davalia_frond_parts.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frond","wikidata":"Q2333821"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Agave_americana_R01.jpg/500px-Agave_americana_R01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Agave_americana_R01.jpg/1280px-Agave_americana_R01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Agave_americana_R01.jpg/500px-Agave_americana_R01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAgave_americana_R01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg/250px-Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg/250px-Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The large flower spike of Agave chiapensis, San Francisco Botanical Garden","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAgave_chiapensis_whole.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Agave_shawii_shawii_rosette_life_cycle.png/500px-Agave_shawii_shawii_rosette_life_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb